---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/CaseStudy/Figure.astro";
import ResultHighlights from "../../components/CaseStudy/ResultHighlights";
import HighlightItem from "../../components/CaseStudy/HighlightItem";
import image_thunderbird_pose from "../../images/ubc-thunderbird/thunderbird_pose.jpg";
import image_thunderbird_sea from "../../images/ubc-thunderbird/thunderbird_sea.jpg";
import image_ubc_sailbot_team from "../../images/ubc-thunderbird/ubc_sailbot_team.jpg";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  slug="ubc-thunderbird"
  heroImage={image_thunderbird_pose}
  heroAlt="Myself posing with Thunderbird during testing"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Thunderbird was a robotic sailboat I revived solo for <a
      href="https://www.ubcsailbot.org/"
    >
      UBC Sailbot
    </a>, restoring an inherited codebase to defend the team's win at the 2014
    International Robotic Sailing Regatta.
  </Fragment>
  <span slot="meta"
    ><i class="bi bi-braces"></i>{" "}Solo developer: revamped and validated the
    inherited codebase</span
  >

  <section class="mb-5">
    <h2>The Problem</h2>
    <p>
      In 2014, I was part of the UBC Sailbot student team, a group that built
      robotic sailboats. Whereas the majority of the team was in design mode for
      a future boat, I alone was tasked to revamp and restore the previous
      year's boat, Thunderbird, to compete in the annual <a
        href="https://www.sailbot.org/">International Robotic Sailing Regatta</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>The main issues with the previous boat were:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The core contributors to the codebase had just graduated, <em
          class="fw-bold">leaving code without meaningful context transfer</em
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        Because of this, code needed to be understood so sponsor presentations
        could come from <em class="fw-bold">a place of genuine knowledge</em>
      </li>
      <li>
        In addition, the IRSR had updated challenges which required <em
          class="fw-bold">new functionality to be implemented</em
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-5">
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <p>
      Luckily, the boat I inherited was already a winning vessel (it won in
      2013). One of the core factors in their success that year was testing with
      a simulated boat. Therefore I set out to ensure the simulation code, as
      well as the controls code, were all understood. I did this by documenting
      code sections systematically on each system, which also created resources
      for knowledge transfer to the rest of the team.
    </p>
    <p>
      To validate my findings, I ran extensive simulation and field testing to
      capture behaviour and reverse-engineer code. Eventually I learned that
      everything was implemented in C on an Arduino for controls while a
      Raspberry Pi handled navigation in Python.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={image_thunderbird_sea}
      alt="Thunderbird sailing autonomously during field testing on open water"
      caption="Field testing Thunderbird's autonomous sailing"
    />
    <p>In summary:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        By being thorough in documenting code behaviour, I created knowledge
        resources that directly supported other members and myself for our
        presentations during demos
      </li>
      <li>
        Frequent testing allowed me to understand the control system and
        implement new behaviours correctly
      </li>
      <li>
        For instance, I expanded on the code by implementing the box challenge,
        where the boat had to hold its position within a specified box
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-5">
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p>
      On competition day, while the team attended the competition, I watched the
      event nervously as the solo developer of the changes to the inherited
      codebase. Fortunately, it was exhilarating to see the boat pass all
      challenges, especially the new box challenge which other teams struggled
      with.
    </p>
    <ResultHighlights>
      <HighlightItem icon="bi-check2-circle">
        <strong>Passed every challenge</strong> — including the new box challenge
        that tripped up other teams
      </HighlightItem>
      <HighlightItem icon="bi-trophy">
        <strong>Perfect 50/50 finish</strong> — continuing UBC Sailbot's win streak
        at IRSR 2014
      </HighlightItem>
      <HighlightItem icon="bi-journal-text">
        <strong>Durable knowledge transfer</strong> — documentation and testing resources
        that outlived the competition
      </HighlightItem>
    </ResultHighlights>
    <Figure
      src={image_ubc_sailbot_team}
      alt="The UBC Sailbot team celebrating their win at the 2014 International Robotic Sailing Regatta"
      caption="The UBC Sailbot team celebrating IRSR 2014"
    />
    <p class="fst-italic">
      I am proud that my contributions led to another perfect 50/50 win at IRSR
      2014, continuing a team streak of perfection. Not only am I grateful to be
      part of that winning legacy, I am honoured to have contributed my
      knowledge to the team in a durable, tangible way.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
